  "findings": [
    "GPU reduction avoids host summation and activation/result round trips; all prefill routes remain intact.",
    "All switches are opt-in. Original greedy and fast profiles are frozen. MTP depth remains three; route-drop and approximate-acceptance bounds are unchanged.",
    "The drop bound limits original routing mass, not expert count or output error; it is never used as a compute speedup multiplier.",
    "The CPU read probe measured 117.816 GB/s, but excludes sparse scale decoding, Q8 dot instructions and scheduling.",
    "Expanded scale metadata improved isolated kernels but lost in full-model screens through cache construction, eviction and memory pressure.",
    "Wider MTP verification, CPU row tiles, expert teams, deeper queues and grouped prefill GEMMs did not win the measured screening workload.",
    "CUDA event intervals include host enqueue gaps and overlap across streams; they are not additive pure kernel durations.",
    "Full-model qualification and fresh coding results are recorded separately from single-screen observations. No held-out tuning.",
    "Prepared ordered four-part KDA prefill measured 520.894 tok/s against the same-binary 454.220 tok/s control, 14.68% faster. Decode 32.099 tok/s versus 31.872 tok/s is not claimed as a general gain.",
